<header id="top" class="modnav" x-data="{ menuOpen: false }">
  <%# ONE row (operator ruling 2026-09-06): brand hard left, nav in the middle,
      Manage Session as the LAST pill of the nav, directly after "More". The wordmark used to be a centred line of its
      own above a centred row of pills, which spent two rows of vertical space
      saying what fits in one. %>
  <%# SESSION STATE IS ALWAYS THE FIRST THING UNDER THE BAR (operator ruling
      2026-09-18): "Any page that attaches a 'search' sub-header is bumping the
      session state header down. session state should always be the first
      element underneath the main header."

      It was, structurally. The strip is absolute at top:100% of its positioned
      ancestor, and that ancestor was .modnav -- which CONTAINS the subnav. So
      100% resolved to the bottom of the whole header, and on every page that
      set :secondary_links the strip descended below the search row instead of
      below the bar. On pages without one it looked correct, which is why it
      survived: the bug was invisible exactly where most people looked.

      This wrapper is the fix. It holds the bar and the strip and nothing else,
      so top:100% means "under the bar" on every page, with or without a
      subnav. The reserved room moves here with it, which also puts the subnav
      BELOW the strip's landing zone rather than on top of it -- the strip
      still occludes nothing when it opens, which is the property three earlier
      cuts were rejected for losing. %>
  <div class="modnav-head">
    <div class="modnav-bar">
    <div class="modnav-brand">
      <%= link_to main_app.root_path, id: "app-logo", class: "modnav-logo" do %>
        <%= image_pack_tag("static/danbooru-logo-128x128.png", alt: "") %>
      <% end %>
      <%= link_to app_name, main_app.root_path, id: "app-name", class: "modnav-wordmark" %>
    </div>

    <nav class="modnav-nav desktop-only" x-bind:class="{ 'desktop-only': !menuOpen }">
      <div class="modnav-links">
        <% entries.each do |entry| %>
          <%# A disabled entry is a SPAN, never a styled anchor. An <a> with a
              class that looks inert is still focusable, still in the tab order,
              still announced as a link, and still followable by anyone who
              reads the markup -- which is the same "looks like a wall, is not
              one" mistake as hiding a control in CSS. No href, no navigation. %>
          <% if entry[:disabled] %>
            <span id="<%= dom_id(entry) %>"
                  class="modnav-pill modnav-pill--<%= entry[:category] %> is-disabled"
                  aria-disabled="true"
                  title="<%= entry[:disabled_reason] %>">
              <span class="modnav-pill-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><%= entry[:label] %>
            </span>
          <% else %>
            <%= link_to entry[:href],
                  id: dom_id(entry),
                  class: "modnav-pill modnav-pill--#{entry[:category]}#{' is-current' if current?(entry)}",
                  rel: entry[:rel] do %>
              <span class="modnav-pill-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><%= entry[:label] %>
              <% if entry[:count].to_i > 0 %>
                <span class="modnav-pill-count"><%= entry[:count] %></span>
              <% end %>
              <%= entry[:badge] %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
        <%# The ONE login/logout point (operator ruling 2026-09-04): a control,
            not a link. Its dot doubles as the summary lamp, so session state is
            readable without opening anything.

            THE STRIP DESCENDS (operator ruling 2026-09-07): a full-width line
            attached under the header, not a panel unfurling along the pill row.
            Three earlier cuts failed, and each failure is why the next one
            looked the way it did. A second header ROW shoved the page down.
            Anchoring the group hard right slid the pill out from under the
            cursor as it opened. Unfurling sideways from the pill gave the
            monitors only the room left over beside "More", so they came out
            jammed together and cut off by the window edge.

            Full width is what fixes that: one line, everything legible at once.
            It is laid OVER the page rather than inserted into it, so nothing
            below moves -- occlusion is not displacement, which is the
            distinction the no-forced-reflow rule turns on. %>
          <button type="button" id="modnav-session-toggle" class="modnav-pill modnav-pill--session"
                  aria-expanded="<%= session_bar_open? %>" aria-controls="modnav-session">
            <span class="modnav-pill-dot" data-region="summary-lamp" aria-hidden="true"></span>Manage Session
          </button>

      </div>
    </nav>


    <%# RELOAD THIS PANEL, on its own.

        This site is normally read inside Technetium's frame, where the
        browser's own reload belongs to the parent document -- there was no way
        to refresh just this panel without leaving the client.

        A plain reload is enough HERE, unlike on the sampling surface: Rails
        fingerprints its assets, so a changed stylesheet arrives under a new
        URL on its own and the page itself is never cached. No stamp is added,
        because a query parameter this app does not expect would ride along
        into pagination and search links. %>
    <button type="button" class="modnav-refresh" x-on:click="location.reload()"
            title="Reload this panel">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path d="M20 12a8 8 0 1 1-2.34-5.66"/>
        <path d="M20 4v4.5h-4.5"/>
      </svg>
      <span class="modnav-refresh-label">Reload this panel</span>
    </button>

    <a class="modnav-burger mobile-only" x-on:click="menuOpen = !menuOpen" aria-label="Menu">
      <%= menu_icon(class: "block w-6", "x-show": "!menuOpen") %>
      <%= close_icon(class: "block w-6", "x-show": "menuOpen", "x-cloak": true) %>
    </a>
    </div>

  <% if observation.present? %>
    <div class="modnav-session" id="modnav-session" data-observation="<%= observation.to_json %>"
         <%= "hidden" unless session_bar_open? %>>
      <div class="modnav-session-inner">
        <div class="modnav-monitor" data-monitor="booru">
          <%# The tip's trigger is the lamp and the service name ONLY. Hovering the
              status text or the sign-in control beside it is not asking what the
              monitor observed (operator, 2026-09-09). tabindex makes the same
              evidence reachable by keyboard, which a hover-only affordance is not. %>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-trigger" data-region="trigger"
                tabindex="0" aria-describedby="modnav-tip-booru">
            <span class="modnav-lamp" data-region="lamp" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <%# A FIXED label. It used to print the cookie name it had read,
                which put "danbooru2 session" in the chrome of every page to
                say something a visitor cannot act on. The read is still the
                monitor's evidence and still shown -- in the tooltip, where
                asking for it is a deliberate act (operator ruling
                2026-09-06). The lamp still turns red when the observed
                object is not the expected one, so the check did not become
                an assertion; only its resting display got quieter. %>
            <span class="modnav-monitor-label">Booru:</span>
          </span>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-status" data-region="status"></span>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-actions" data-region="controls"></span>
          <div class="modnav-monitor-tip" id="modnav-tip-booru" data-region="tip" role="tooltip"></div>
        </div>
        <span class="modnav-monitor-sep" aria-hidden="true">|</span>
        <div class="modnav-monitor" data-monitor="matrix">
          <%# The tip's trigger is the lamp and the service name ONLY. Hovering the
              status text or the sign-in control beside it is not asking what the
              monitor observed (operator, 2026-09-09). tabindex makes the same
              evidence reachable by keyboard, which a hover-only affordance is not. %>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-trigger" data-region="trigger"
                tabindex="0" aria-describedby="modnav-tip-matrix">
            <span class="modnav-lamp" data-region="lamp" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="modnav-monitor-label">Matrix:</span>
          </span>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-status" data-region="status"></span>
          <span class="modnav-monitor-actions" data-region="controls"></span>
          <div class="modnav-monitor-tip" id="modnav-tip-matrix" data-region="tip" role="tooltip"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
  </div><%# /.modnav-head %>

  <% if content_for?(:secondary_links) %>
    <div class="modnav-subnav">
      <div class="modnav-subnav-inner"><%= content_for :secondary_links %></div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</header>
